96
00:13:16.370 --> 00:13:30.239
marco: Cool, so this is an extension to Gossip Sub that helps remove the head-of-line blocking issues. I'll explain a bit more about that, and avoids… lets us avoid the topic name overhead.

97
00:13:30.710 --> 00:13:31.590
marco: So…

98
00:13:32.530 --> 00:13:48.860
marco: In our gossip that we send to peers, we have a very different set of messages that we send. We have big messages and small messages. We have the big block messages that have, like, the whole payload, and then we have, like, very small attestation and aggregate information.

99
00:13:49.440 --> 00:13:53.360
marco: And all of these are put into a single stream.

100
00:13:53.920 --> 00:14:02.740
marco: Even if the transport… all our transports support multiplexing, we don't have… we don't take advantage of that. Everything is just serialized on that one stream.

101
00:14:03.450 --> 00:14:14.530
marco: So what that means is if you have a small message, and it's stuck behind a big message, you have to wait for that big message to be sent out before that small message can be read by the peer.

102
00:14:17.060 --> 00:14:22.530
marco: So… The ordering is useful, but really it's only useful within a topic.

103
00:14:23.390 --> 00:14:27.550
marco: And we don't want… we don't really care about ordering across topics.

104
00:14:28.550 --> 00:14:33.890
marco: And the control messages are very nice to have ordered. It just makes implementation simpler.

105
00:14:36.030 --> 00:14:41.579
marco: And we don't want this to be, like, a massive change that everyone has to organize.

106
00:14:42.040 --> 00:14:45.289
marco: And, synchronize to deploy.

107
00:14:46.610 --> 00:14:53.680
marco: And while we're at it, we can also avoid sending the topic name on every message. And this is a little subtle if you're not thinking about the wire.

108
00:14:53.800 --> 00:14:56.710
marco: Details, but here's a… let me…

109
00:14:57.510 --> 00:15:01.980
marco: break it down a bit. If you have the attestation, it's a very small message.

110
00:15:03.570 --> 00:15:17.210
marco: But the topic name is, like, this really big thing that has, like, ETH2, whatever, whatever, subnet, whatever, and then the encoding. And this thing is a sizable part of that whole message, like, if the message is, like.

111
00:15:17.600 --> 00:15:25.360
marco: I don't know, 500 bytes, and this thing is… just the topic name is about, you know, 10 bytes, so…

112
00:15:26.250 --> 00:15:28.140
marco: Yeah, a big part of it.

113
00:15:28.940 --> 00:15:30.460
marco: And we can, we can avoid that.

114
00:15:31.120 --> 00:15:40.020
marco: So… That's what this extension does. It's about splitting messages into… the topic messages into separate streams.

115
00:15:41.520 --> 00:15:45.770
marco: So each topic gets its own long-lived stream.

116
00:15:46.000 --> 00:15:53.540
marco: And at the very beginning of that stream, we send a header that says, this is the topic name for this stream.

117
00:15:53.900 --> 00:15:57.920
marco: And then messages Topic messages just go on that stream.

118
00:15:58.810 --> 00:16:04.379
marco: It allows interleaving of big messages and small messages. This works, obviously, with Quick.

119
00:16:04.560 --> 00:16:12.820
marco: But it also works with our existing muxers on TCP. So yamux will interleave frames for multiple streams.

120
00:16:12.980 --> 00:16:16.230
marco: I'm pretty sure MPLEX does this, too.

121
00:16:16.790 --> 00:16:21.029
marco: Yeah, so even with all our transports, we get this benefit.

122
00:16:21.810 --> 00:16:26.189
marco: This is the rough shape. We… both peers advertised this extension.

123
00:16:26.780 --> 00:16:28.630
marco: And then they can open the…

124
00:16:28.770 --> 00:16:32.850
marco: Topic stream protocol, which is GSTS beta.

125
00:16:32.990 --> 00:16:36.640
marco: They specify what the topic name is, and then they publish

126
00:16:36.900 --> 00:16:45.369
marco: the RPC messages on that topic stream. They omit the topic name in those RPC messages because they don't need it.

127
00:16:48.760 --> 00:16:51.889
marco: Yeah, so again, this gives you the…

128
00:16:52.810 --> 00:17:05.010
marco: idea of what's happening on the wire. So, before, you would see on the left, we would send this topic name every time along with the data. On the right, now we could just send that topic name once, and the data is really compact.

129
00:17:08.480 --> 00:17:16.669
marco: So, yeah, this solves the head-of-line blocking, reduces the overhead for topic names. There's a couple other issues it doesn't solve, right? If it's…

130
00:17:17.420 --> 00:17:23.030
marco: You're gonna have the same… Messages within a topic be serialized, so we're not…

131
00:17:23.500 --> 00:17:31.140
marco: Avoiding that, but that's actually kind of the intent, because it might… it's simpler to reason about a model where topics come serially.

132
00:17:31.610 --> 00:17:33.229
marco: And we don't have any

133
00:17:33.500 --> 00:17:40.299
marco: stream priorities here, although you could imagine adding stream priorities would go really well with this, because you might set

134
00:17:40.600 --> 00:17:47.719
marco: At the stations have really high priority, and then the payload body have a lower priority.

135
00:17:51.250 --> 00:17:55.140
marco: Yeah, I think we can skip through this. So…

136
00:17:55.360 --> 00:18:01.890
marco: I will post this link… I'll also post the whole this whole slide. But…

137
00:18:02.220 --> 00:18:16.310
marco: Spec 729 has the topic streams working draft, the detailed specification of this change, of this gossip sub-extension. We have an implementation already in Go, and I have an interop test.

138
00:18:16.670 --> 00:18:24.630
marco: that will let people make sure that their implementation works. It's built on Shadow, so it's agnostic to the actual,

139
00:18:24.850 --> 00:18:29.109
marco: Implementation. And the way it works is… It's wolves.

140
00:18:29.210 --> 00:18:35.539
marco: Concurrently publish… well, it will publish a 1MB message on one topic, and then a small message on another top.

141
00:18:35.940 --> 00:18:42.459
marco: And it'll look at the timestamps in the log, and it fails if the small message gets blocked behind the big message.

142
00:18:43.300 --> 00:18:52.250
marco: Yeah, so this… this test passes on the Go implementation and then fails on without top extremes.

143
00:18:54.200 --> 00:19:03.260
marco: So… like, taking a step back, basically, what we're trying to do is everything right now has to go through a single queue.

144
00:19:03.800 --> 00:19:07.450
marco: Topic streams, let's… Everyone get their own cue.

145
00:19:10.040 --> 00:19:16.899
marco: there's some future work that could be interesting, so we've talked a little bit about this in the past at Gossip Sub.

146
00:19:17.040 --> 00:19:21.540
marco: A message header that tells you what the message ID is that you're about to receive.

147
00:19:21.800 --> 00:19:27.399
marco: This would allow the receiver to do something about it, instead of just having to wait to receive the whole message.

148
00:19:28.920 --> 00:19:43.139
marco: a way for the sender to reset that topic stream to drop a message mid-flight, so you can imagine if I'm giving you, like, a really big message, and you tell me, hey, I already have that message, there's no point in me finish… finishing sending that message. I should just…

149
00:19:43.870 --> 00:19:46.299
marco: Drop that stream, and then open a new one.

150
00:19:47.100 --> 00:20:05.100
marco: There's also, like, some interesting work we could do here with the receiver setting flow control to the throttle peers, right? Because, like, we might say, for this subset of my mesh peers, I want to throttle them down so they can't send me a ton of data right away, but for these other peers, you know, I…

151
00:20:05.280 --> 00:20:08.780
marco: Want them to be able to send me data as soon as they get it.

152
00:20:11.910 --> 00:20:16.230
marco: That's it. That's my super quick presentation on topic streams.

